rio de janeiro with its harbor like a  fallen star  and the curve of its beaches with their  names like songs  flamingo votofogo copacabana rio  isn't what it was these days  it's a city emblematic of a country in  trouble  there are signs of corruption everywhere  from the football stadium rebuilt for  the world cup with dirty money  to the bay choked with pollution because  corrupt officials never cleaned it  then there's the violence last month 28  was shot dead by the city's police  in the bloodiest favela raid in rio's  history  and to top it off the city's hospitals  are struggling to cope with covert 19.  this is just one city in a country of  more than 211  million people but the problems it faces  are felt nationwide i think it's going  to be a really  complicated and tense couple of years  with something that the whole world  should be paying attention to  despite all this thousands of brazilians  were on the street last month  to show their support for right-wing  president jair bolsonaro  a former army captain famous for his  racist  sexist and homophobic comments  his actions are now threatening not only  brazilian democracy  but one of the world's most important  natural resources  so why are his followers still so  faithful  and could the country withstand another  four years of his leadership  coronavirus has taken a terrible toll on  brazil  in april the country suffered the  highest rate of covert deaths  of any nation on earth and many blame  president jaye bolsonaro  for letting it get this bad  he famously dismissed the virus as  nothing more than flu  that wouldn't affect a strong man like  him  and as hospitals ran out of oxygen he  refused to order lockdowns  and even joked the vaccine could turn  people into crocodiles  bolsonaro's early response to the covid  crisis  was to shrug it off to say that  you know covert wasn't a big deal and of  course that hasn't proved  to be true and covid has killed one in  every 500 brazilians  the number is absolutely catastrophic  despite this in the early months of the  pandemic  bolsonaro's popularity soared this was  because his government issued emergency  payments  to keep people out of poverty it was one  of the biggest welfare programs in  brazil's history  without it the number of people living  on the breadline  would have more than doubled whereas by  the end of 2020  a million people had been lifted out of  poverty  these policies led to almost god-like  worship of bolsonaro  among some people  this is bahaji pajuka a town es salvador  in the northeast of brazil it's one of  the poorest parts of the country  with the highest rates of unemployment  but that doesn't matter to senor antonio  his family runs the town fish store and  he is a local politician  and passionate bolsonaro supporters  do you want to  is just one of the tens of millions of  brazilians who voted for bolsonaro in  2018  when he was elected with 55 percent of  the vote  but just 10 years ago it would have been  unthinkable  that someone like bolsonaro would take  power here  brazil was riding high the country was  led by a progressive government  headed by a woman jilma hussefi  it was slated to host the upcoming world  cup and olympics  and touted as a future economic  powerhouse alongside the other brics  economies  russia india china and south africa  the economy was booming over the course  of 2010  gdp grew by 7.5 percent and poverty was  falling  but then things started to change in  2014  the country fell into a deep and lasting  recession  partly caused by the end of the  commodities boom and financial  mismanagement  by ms husefi's workers party  the main consequence of the economic  crisis was uh  this uh perception this increased  perception that the political class was  um  inefficient and and didn't have the  capacity to deal  with those problems alongside this  the workers party or pt was engulfed in  a huge corruption scandal  known as lavagiato or car wash after the  petrol station where it was first  uncovered  you know it wasn't a huge surprise to  people that there was corruption going  on but what was  a massive shock was the scale of it  dozens of companies were bribing the  state oil firm  petrobras to get government contracts  and that that money was then being  funneled to  all sorts of different political parties  into this  came bolsonaro a little-known  congressman who made much of his  military credentials as a former army  captain  he promised to bring back order to the  corrupt system  what elected him in the end wasn't so  much the policies that he promised  but really this rebellion against what  had been there before him  we are  bolsonaro also borrowed heavily from  donald trump's playbook  [Applause]  like trump he used social media to rally  support  and position himself as a plain speaking  man of the people  even if his views were deeply offensive  [Music]  he appealed to conservative voters by  promising to restore family values  and key to his election success were  evangelical churches  brazil is the world's largest catholic  country  but evangelical protestantism is  predicted to become the biggest religion  here  in just over 10 years time  back in bahaji worshipers at one local  church are among the many evangelicals  who voted for him cremilde  and has been a member of the church  since she was 7.  and she was proud to vote for bolsonaro  as a man of faith  but now he's been in power for a few  years kumilda is starting to question if  bolsonaro is the best man to lead brazil  is not alone in her assessment in recent  months  bolsonaro's popularity rating has  dropped to below 30 percent  the lowest since he came to power  this is partly because the economic aid  in the early days of the pandemic  has now dried up and the reality of  brazil's economic situation  is setting in despite gdp recovering  more strongly than expected  unemployment has hit an 18-year high and  now that the government payments have  stopped  the poverty rate is higher than it has  been for over a decade  but bolsonaro shows little enthusiasm  for the kind of interventions needed  to get the economy back on track  so in bolsonaro's first year in office  they did manage to do  a really important pensions reform but  then after that the other reforms sort  of stalled  he was done doing unpopular economic  proposals  and um and so you know this whole  economic agenda  really just stayed on the table  but bolsonaro's policy decisions do not  just affect brazil  some have a bearing on the welfare of  the entire planet  brazil is home to the amazon rainforest  and although deforestation in the amazon  had been rising since 2012  it shot up when he took office  a lot of the damage that has been done  it's irreparable  he cut the budget of the environmental  ministry  he you know forced out a lot of the most  competent staff  and then he also sent a really um  you know a really powerful message to  people who are involved in deforestation  which was that  it's okay with me and the the effect of  that has just been catastrophic  his record on corruption is equally dire  [Music]  two of his sons are being investigated  for corruption  this has led him to abandon  anti-corruption reforms  and make deals with parties tainted by  the lava jato scandal  in the hope this will save his sons from  prison and himself  from impeachment the question is is this  damage that can be  that can be repairable is this you know  the kind of a  situation that someone else can come in  and sort of  fix what he has broken  and i think that this is really the main  question going forward  brazil's democracy is still young and  there are positive signs that it will  survive bolsonaro  the country's institutions have managed  to hold him in check  congress and the supreme court have  blocked some of his more extreme  proposals  such as allowing gun-owning brazilians  to openly carry firearms  and the pandemic has left him looking  particularly vulnerable  congress is now investigating his  handling of covert 19  which could hurt bolsonaro in the next  election and people are on the streets  calling for his impeachment  i don't think bolsonaro is going to be  able to  to undermine democratic institutions in  the country because what we've been  observing is that he  as he attacks democracy and and  and and opposes democratic institutions  he also  he's getting progressively more isolated  he's having a really hard time  governing bolsonaro has tried to shore  up his power through the military  but they may soon have to choose between  him and democracy  the next election in 2022 promises to be  a political showdown  as luis inacio lula da silva or lula  the most popular brazilian president to  date has hinted he will run again  but bolsonaro has already indicated that  should he lose the election  he would not go quietly and the events  that played out on capitol hill earlier  this year  could be repeated in brazil when you ask  could what happened in the u.s at the  capitol building happen in brazil  i think the answer is absolutely and  what we don't know is whether it would  turn out the same way whether in the end  the democratic institutions would be  able to sort of take control  so while brazil has so far survived  bolsonaro's onslaught  the greatest challenge to its democracy  may well be yet to come  and those on the ground are simply  praying that things will get better  his legacy is going to be you know a  tragic one  i hope that it's one that brazil can  recover from  that you know brazilian institutions can  start to pick up  the pieces and and heal from this i  think  they will but i think it's going to be a  really  complicated and tense couple of years  and you know it's something that the  whole world should be paying attention  to  [Music]  hi i'm sarah maslin i am the brazil  correspondent for the economist  if you would like to learn more about  brazil under zaire 